# B819: models set does not persist across daemon restarts; add durable session/room model persistence

User expectation: `models set` on a session should survive daemon restarts by
default (or a room-profile fallback should apply automatically). Currently a
daemon restart resets the session model to the default.

Observed 2026-09-24: user set session model, daemon signal-restarted for the
Teams PR feed rollout, session model reset.

Options:
- Persist selected model in session_state so sessions resume with it.
- Or auto-create/update a room profile binding when the user sets a model.

Workaround in place: room profile "telegram-admin" pinned for the Telegram DM.


[Decision 2026-09-25, coordinator] Persistence layer exists (session_state.model_override, Memory.set_session_model_override) and session-create reads it via resolve_model_for_session tier1, but the reset was still observed after signal-restart. Root-cause empirically: write a restart-simulation regression test first (Session manager A over file DB, set model, new manager B over same DB, get_or_create, assert effective model). If that passes, hunt the leak (Session.turn ~model overrides, pending-session resume path, key sanitization) with the same test style. Deliverable: failing-then-passing test + fix at the actual leak. Live DB confirms overrides persist (telegram:58495225:58495225 = zai_coding:glm-5.3-flash).

[Findings 2026-09-25, b822-model-persist] (1) TDD restart simulation over a temp FILE-backed DB (two Session managers, get_or_create via runtime_context_block) shows the core mechanism is sound: the DB tier-1 override applies on session load and survives restart; update_config (SIGHUP/config-set reload) with a CHANGED global default also re-applies the override correctly. Also verified: no Session.turn caller passes ~model; daemon_resume/inbound_replay go through with_session_lock so tier-1 applies at load; every agent_defaults.primary_model writer is override-aware or auxiliary. (2) REAL leak, demonstrated with a failing test (test_model_override_set_uses_sanitized_key): key-sanitization duality. Session.turn/with_session_lock/get_or_create_locked sanitize keys ('/'->'_'), but Session.set_session_model/_with_compact/clear/get_session_model_override used the caller key verbatim. The models tool receives the RAW key in Tool.session_key (github webhook keys are github:owner/repo:pr:N), so 'models set' wrote session_state.model_override under the raw key and missed the in-memory session: the running session kept the old model and the sanitized tier-1 lookup never found the row, so after any restart the session reset to the default. Fix: sanitize the key at the Session model-override API boundary (set/set_with_compact/clear/get_override/get_effective); this also repairs the Restart_notify update-marker restore path. (3) Caveat: the reported telegram incident key (telegram:58495225:58495225) is sanitize-clean and its override row persists; no telegram-path leak reproduces, so that specific observation may have been a different session (e.g. a github PR session) or the override being set after the incident. Regression guards added: file-DB restart, restart+config-reload, and raw-key set tests in test_session_model_override.ml.